You can represent each node as a dict with a special key (e.g., "_end") to mark the end of a word. Insertion walks the characters, creating a new dict when a child is missing; search follows the same path and checks the end marker. A minimal example:
```python
class Trie:
    def __init__(self):
        self.root = {}
    def insert(self, word):
        node = self.root
        for ch in word:
            node = node.setdefault(ch, {})
        node['_end'] = True
    def search(self, word):
        node = self.root
        for ch in word:
            if ch not in node:
                return False
            node = node[ch]
        return '_end' in node
```

__repr__ is the special method Python calls to get the official string representation of an object.  If you define __repr__(self) to return a helpful description, print(my_obj) (or just typing the variable in the REPL) will show that instead of the default memory address.  Example:

class Point:
    def __init__(self, x, y):
        self.x, self.y = x, y
    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Point(x={self.x}, y={self.y})"

Now Point(1,2) prints as Point(x=1, y=2).  __repr__ should aim to be unambiguous and, if possible, eval‑able, whereas __str__ is for a user‑friendly format.

Implement the __eq__(self, other) method to compare the relevant attributes and return a Boolean.  Python will then use it for the == operator.  Example:

class Card:
    def __init__(self, rank, suit):
        self.rank, self.suit = rank, suit
    def __eq__(self, other):
        if not isinstance(other, Card):
            return NotImplemented
        return self.rank == other.rank and self.suit == other.suit

Now Card('A','♠') == Card('A','♠') evaluates to True.

