Reversing a singly linked list in place means re‑linking the `next` pointers so the list runs backward, using only O(1) extra space. The classic iterative method keeps three pointers: `prev`, `curr`, and `next`. While traversing, you store `curr->next` in `next`, set `curr->next = prev`, then move `prev` and `curr` forward. After the loop, `prev` points to the new head. In C:
```c
Node *prev = NULL, *curr = head, *next;
while (curr) { next = curr->next; curr->next = prev; prev = curr; curr = next; }
head = prev;
```

