Use `std::unique_ptr` for sole ownership and `std::shared_ptr` when multiple objects need to share ownership. For a singly linked list, `std::unique_ptr<Node>` for the `next` pointer works well because each node owns its successor and the chain is automatically destroyed when the head goes out of scope. Example:
```cpp
struct Node { int val; std::unique_ptr<Node> next; };
auto head = std::make_unique<Node>();
head->next = std::make_unique<Node>();
```
Avoid raw `new/delete` unless you have a very good reason; smart pointers eliminate most leaks.

