answer:
A deadlock occurs when each thread holds a lock the other thread needs and none can proceed, forming a circular wait. If Thread A locks mutex X then tries to lock mutex Y while Thread B has already locked Y and then tries to lock X, both will wait forever. The classic fix is to enforce a consistent lock acquisition order, e.g., always lock the lower‑addressed mutex first:
```c
if (addr1 < addr2) {
    lock(m1);
    lock(m2);
} else {
    lock(m2);
    lock(m1);
}
```This eliminates the circular wait condition.

