Mergesort recursively splits the input array in half until each sub‑array has one element, which is trivially sorted. Then it merges the sorted halves by repeatedly taking the smallest front element from the two sub‑arrays and appending it to a new array. The merge step runs in linear time because each element is examined exactly once. The recursion depth is log₂ n, so the overall running time is O(n log n). In Python a tiny version looks like:

```python
def mergesort(a):
    if len(a) <= 1:
        return a
    mid = len(a)//2
    left = mergesort(a[:mid])
    right = mergesort(a[mid:])
    return merge(left, right)
```

__repr__ is the special method that Python calls to get the official string representation of an object. If you don’t define it, the default implementation from object is used, which prints the class name and memory address. Implementing __repr__ lets you return a helpful string, e.g.:
```python
class Point:
    def __init__(self, x, y):
        self.x, self.y = x, y
    def __repr__(self):
        return f"Point({self.x}, {self.y})"
```
