Equality (__eq__) alone is not enough for hash‑based collections; they also require a matching __hash__ implementation. If you define __eq__ without defining __hash__, Python sets __hash__ to None, making instances unhashable, which means they cannot be stored in a set or used as dict keys. Define both methods consistently, e.g.:
```python
class Person:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
    def __eq__(self, other):
        return isinstance(other, Person) and self.name == other.name
    def __hash__(self):
        return hash(self.name)
```

