The sliding window technique is a way to process a contiguous subset of elements in a data structure (usually an array or string) while moving the subset forward one step at a time. It is most useful when you need to compute something like a sum, maximum, or count over all subarrays of a fixed size, or when you need to find the smallest subarray that satisfies a condition. By keeping track of the window's start and end indices, you can update the result in O(1) time as the window moves, giving an overall O(n) solution instead of O(n × k). For example, to find the maximum sum of any 3‑element window you can do:

int sum = a[0] + a[1] + a[2];
int max = sum;
for (int i = 3; i < n; ++i) {
    sum += a[i] - a[i-3]; // slide window forward
    max = Math.max(max, sum);
}

